==English==

===Etymology===
{{compound|en|roll|over}}, from the verb phrase.

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}
{{wikipedia}}

# The process of [[increment]]ing, especially back to an initial value.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2011|author=Jeff Ryan|title=Super Mario: How Nintendo Conquered America|passage=For ''Donkey Kong'', the '''rollover''' on board 117 causes a “kill screen”—Mario is simply not given enough time to complete the level before time runs out.}}
# {{lb|en|automotive}} A [[road]] [[traffic]] [[accident]] in which a [[vehicle]] [[overturn]]s.
# {{lb|en|computing}} A [[graphic]] [[element]] that changes its appearance when the [[cursor]] moves over it.
# {{lb|en|computing}} A [[keyboard]] [[feature]] where each [[key]] is [[scan]]ned independently, so that multiple simultaneous keypresses always register correctly.
# {{lb|en|finance}} The [[reinvestment]] of [[fund]]s in a new [[issue]] of the same or similar [[investment]].
# {{lb|en|finance}} A [[fee]] paid by a [[borrower]] in order to [[defer]] full [[repayment]] of a [[loan]].
# {{lb|en|British}} In the National or European lottery, the situation in which a [[jackpot]] that has not been [[won]] is [[carried]] [[over]] to the next [[week]].
# {{lb|en|pinball}} A [[target]] on the [[pinball]] table that is [[activate]]d when the [[ball]] rolls over it.
#* '''2010''', Bob LeVitus, ''Incredible iPhone Apps For Dummies'' (page 78)
#*: One thing I hate about some pinball games is that they don't let you know your current objective, what happens when you hit specific targets or '''rollovers''', or what targets you should be aiming for right now.

